{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head fcadbb228a7d0245fb2c94559e3672c9b55dc89c, sole artifact notes/2026-10-01-artroom-plan.md.\n\nArchitecture: the durable reservation transaction supplies the missing ordering point. Parallel preparation and serial publication remain appropriate. Recovery must not release the publication slot on an unproven external-writer assumption.\nSecurity: admission-time revocation and the whole-tree default for checks resolve the corresponding findings from review 8233c002. Two contradictory statements about evidence and compromised-key handling need clarification.\nSimplification: the full functional scope is preserved. A visibly unresolved publication is a valid safe state; do not invent a time-based safety guarantee merely to keep the queue moving.\n\nRequired corrections\n\n1. P1 — Token expiry does not establish termination of an already authenticated push (lines 664–678, 688, 718–721).\nA receive-pack request can authenticate while its token is valid and finish after revocation or expiry. The official token API documents revocation, but does not establish cancellation of an in-flight Git operation: https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/api/workers-binding/. This is an unresolved platform behavior to measure, not a guarantee I can infer from the API.\nThe fallback \"wait for the token's expiry\" is therefore insufficient. Stopping the client sandbox also need not cancel work already accepted by the remote. Holding the slot until a confirmed terminal outcome is known is safe; releasing it after an elapsed time is safe only with a proved remote processing bound or receiver-side fence.\nFor example, A remains remotely pending after its token expires. The room labels A not landed and releases the slot; B then reserves against the same main and starts a push. A commits first. B's ref lease prevents a lost update, but the room has misreported A and ordered two reservations against an unresolved write. The no-lost-update guarantee does not establish accurate landing receipts or publication ordering.\nReplace the fallback with \"hold the publication slot in an explicit unresolved state until completion/termination is established; expose it to admins.\" Require the Sandbox spike or lane B to identify what evidence establishes a terminal outcome, and only then enable automated slot release. The published plan can state this fail-closed behavior now without waiting for the live spike. Keep the token revocation/expiry tests, including a delayed already-authenticated request beyond expiry.\n\n2. P2 — Make evidence validity and emergency revocation consistent (lines 626–631, 638–647, 767–789).\nReservation currently requires every evidence actor's current membership/key/delegation, while retired-key evidence is explicitly preserved. Validate new admissions and the land initiator against current authority, and validate earlier evidence against its recorded admission authority plus the stated current evidence-validity policy. Do not accidentally reopen all earlier reviews because their signer retired or a short delegation expired.\nSection 8 says reservation is irrevocable and subsequent acts cannot cancel it. Section 9 says the landing proceeds but also requires revocation to refuse an unfinished push after compromise. State one coherent emergency rule: ordinary post-reservation acts follow the reserved landing; a compromise may attempt an explicit best-effort abort, which has its own recorded terminal outcome if no publication occurred, or opens a revert lane if publication occurred. Do not guarantee remote cancellation. Update the paused-push acceptance cases to distinguish retired from compromised revocation.\n\nNonblocking implementation gates:\n- Scoped checker reuse requires actual input isolation: no canonical repository token or remote, unfiltered Git history/object database, shared cache or filesystem path exposing omitted files. Deliver a filtered snapshot in an isolated workspace and test attempted access to excluded data. A filtered checkout alone is not proof of confinement. Disable reuse for undeclared volatile external inputs. Keep scoped reuse as a feature with these requirements.\n- In the illustrative test runner, check npm ci's exit status before running npm test; a fulfilled exec promise may represent a failing command. Use the actual Sandbox SDK signature or clearly label argument-array execution as an Artroom wrapper. Resolve this in lane 0/runner implementation, without delaying the plan for runnable sample code.\n\nValidation: examined the complete revision-2 baseline and every revision-3 change, including its exact numbered publication and authority sections. No application tests, deployment or source changes were performed. Revision 3 is close to a sound implementation plan. The next plan head can resolve the required corrections through explicit safety and outcome rules; no functionality cut or new scope is requested.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:080e1875d58395cc42d1c249788042bc57594f3e","binding":"assigned","head":"fcadbb228a7d0245fb2c94559e3672c9b55dc89c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9d9c25879df31abdfcb57f947fcc42fc64b66158\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2bb1a40df6c55cf3c724fd05fe6ac9ece18472c1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9d9c25879df31abdfcb57f947fcc42fc64b66158","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}